1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. O-O Be7 7. Re1 (7. e5 {is more popular and regarded as better at this time.}) 7... b5 8. e5 Nxe5 9. Nxe5 bxa4 10. Qxd4 O-O 11. Qxa4 Rb8 12. c4 $5 {I think this is a new move, not sure it's any good, though.} Re8 13. Nc3 Bd6 14. Bf4 Nh5 15. c5 Bxc5 16. Qc4 Re6 $1 17. Nd5 Bd6 18. Nd3 Nxf4 19. N3xf4 Rxe1+ 20. Rxe1 Bb7 21. Qd4 Bc6 22. g3 $1 (22. Nh5 $2 Qg5 23. Nhf6+ gxf6 24. Nxf6+ Kh8 {doesn't achieve anything.}) 22... Qg5 23. h4 Qf5 24. Kh2 Kf8 $2 (24... h6 {is much safer.}) 25. Qa7 Ra8 26. Nxc7 {(Ouch!)} Rc8 27. Qe3 g6 $2 (27... f6 $1 {gives Black reasonable chances.}) 28. Qd4 Kg8 {Otherwise 29 Qh8 mate is strong.} 29. Qxd6 Qa5 30. Re8+ (30. b4 Qxc7 31. Re8+ $1 {was my original intention but I found the following forced mate.}) 30... Rxe8 31. Nxe8 Qe1 (31... Qb6 32. Nf6+ Kg7 33. Ng4 {? Nh5+}) (31... Qd8 32. Nf6+ {just leaves Black a piece down.}) 32. Nf6+ Kg7 33. N4h5+ gxh5 34. Nxh5+ Kg8 35. Qb8+ {Source: Conor O'Shaughnessy in 'Dinosaur' (Mitcham CC bulletin, no.21)} 1-0

