1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 e6 3. c4 c5 4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 g6 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 g5 9. Bg3 Nh5 10. e3 Nxg3 11. hxg3 Bg7 12. Bb5+ $5 $146 {The reason this is a novelty is because no one has been stupid enough to consider playing such a poor move. Normally the bishop does well against the weakened kingside.} Bd7 (12... Nd7) 13. a4 O-O 14. Qd3 $1 (14. Qc2) 14... f5 15. Nd2 a6 16. Bxd7 Nxd7 17. f4 $14 {Tseitlin, but ?/? O'Shaughnessy (at the time) & Stockfish 17 (now).} Qe7 18. a5 Nf6 {(! - White hasn't played the opening very well and I was feeling very happy with this position. He should keep the white-squared bishops on, which increases his pressure and my cramped position - COS)} 19. fxg5 $1 Ng4 $1 ({This hardly qualifies as a sacrifice;} 19... hxg5 $2 20. Qxf5) 20. gxh6 Bxh6 21. Ra4 $1 {Tseitlin seems to be playing a lot of very good moves and getting a very bad position!} Rf6 (21... Nxe3 $2 22. Rxh6 Ng4+ 23. Re6 $18) (21... Bxe3 22. Rxg4+ Bg5+ (22... fxg4 $2 23. Qg6+ Qg7 24. Qe6+ Rf7 (24... Qf7 25. Qxg4+ $18) 25. Qxe3 $18) 23. Kd1 fxg4 24. Qg6+ Qg7 25. Qe6+ Rf7 26. Nde4 $16) ({CO'S adds:} 21... Qxe3+ {Neither of us really considered this move although it seems reasonably good for Black.}) 22. Rxg4+ $1 ({This is probably the best move to try and hang on here.} 22. e4 Nf2 {is extremely painful.}) 22... fxg4 23. Nce4 Rg6 {I offered a draw here on the basis of a good position and not wishing to put the norm in jeopardy.} 24. Nc4 (24. Rh4 $5) 24... Kg7 25. Qc3+ Kg8 26. Qd3 Kg7 27. Qc3+ {Obviously reconsidering.} Kg8 {Sources: MS Tseitlin, Informator 57/88 (non-text annotations); Conor O'Shaughnessy (text annotations in 'Dinosaur' (Mitcham CC bulletin, no.21)} 1/2-1/2

