1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 {This line will always be associated with Fischer and I noticed he had another good win with it against Spassky. I appears unambitious but gets very good results.} dxc6 5. O-O Ne7 {I hadn't seen this before but it doesn't look that great. I decided just to develop with tempo.} 6. Nxe5 Qd4 7. Nf3 (7. Qh5 {(The normal move)} g6 8. Qg5 Bg7 9. Nd3 f5 10. e5 c5 11. b3 $5 h6 12. Qg3 f4 13. Qf3 Bf5 14. Qxb7 Be4 15. Qxc7 Bxd3 16. cxd3 Bxe5 17. Qb7 Rb8 18. Qxa6 f3 $1 19. Nc3 fxg2 20. Re1 O-O 21. Qe6+ Rf7 22. Nd1 Qxa1 23. Qxe5 Qxe5 24. Rxe5 Nc6 25. Rxc5 Nb4 26. Ba3 Nxd3 27. Rc6 Ra8 28. Rd6 Rxa3 29. Rxd3 Rxa2 {Timman-Short, Candidates Final 1993 (9) and Black went on to win}) 7... Qxe4 8. Nc3 Qg6 9. Ne5 Qf5 10. Re1 Be6 11. d4 h5 (11... O-O-O $4 12. g4 {Resigns, was my preferred option}) 12. Ne4 {In the interests of norms I offered a draw here. By the way, this is apparently Rozentalis-Piket, Groningen 1992, and is assessed equal/boring.} O-O-O 13. Nc5 Nd5 14. Nxe6 Qxe6 15. Bg5 $1 Re8 16. Nd3 Qd7 17. Rxe8+ Qxe8 18. Qf3 f6 $6 ({He offered a draw here but I felt I was significantly better.} 18... Nb4 {is much better as more wood disappears.}) 19. Bd2 Qd7 20. Re1 Nb6 21. Qe4 Bd6 $6 (21... Nc4) 22. b3 Rd8 23. c4 Bf8 24. Bc3 Kb8 25. h3 Nc8 26. Qg6 h4 27. Qe4 g5 $6 {It's difficult to see how Black won't be two pawns down in some endgame after this move.} 28. Qg6 Be7 29. Nc5 Qe8 $6 30. Qxe8 Rxe8 31. Nd7+ Ka7 32. Nxf6 Rd8 33. Ng4 b5 34. Ne5 Kb7 35. Re3 Bf6 36. Nxc6 {Source: Conor O'Shaughnessy in 'Dinosaur' (Mitcham CC bulletin, no.21)} 1-0

